    "result_text": "The sheet now has a late percent, and overdue claims gain a late charge. `bin/e2e` passes.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Setting:** the Settings panel has a new \"Late percent\" field showing the current value. It defaults to 0.00. A change is checked like the floor (0.00 or more, up to two decimals), and the server writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=late-pct value=<percent>`. It's stored as another key in the existing settings table, so no migration was needed.\n- **Live due:** for an overdue claim, the app counts the full 30-day spans between its due-by date and the sheet's today. It then adds late percent \u00d7 floored due \u00d7 spans, rounded like the due. One shared hook in `features/claims/claims.ts` now feeds this due to the claim row, due-totals, overdue-totals and period totals, so they all agree.\n- **Settling:** `SettleClaim.java` adds the same late charge on the server before saving. The charge is fixed at that point, so it appears in `CLAIM_SETTLED \u2026 due=` and later late-percent changes don't affect it. A settling clerk is checked against the clerk limit using the due including the late charge.\n\n**Two judgement calls**\n- **Rounding:** I round the total charge once (percent \u00d7 spans) rather than rounding each 30-day span separately. The test can't tell the two apart, but they could differ by a cent on some amounts.\n- **Mismatch on halves (existing, not changed):** the screen rounds exact halves to even, but the server rounds them up. So a claim's due on screen can differ by a cent from the due frozen when it's settled. The late charge follows the same split. I left this alone because it's outside this change.",
